          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Erlangen program
            (in German,
            %em Erlanger Programm
            ) is a project, begun by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Klein"} Felix Klein
            at Erlangen in the 19th century (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872
            ), to study
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            of “geometric shapes”, hence from the perspective of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+spaces"} Euclidean spaces
            but more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+spaces"} homogeneous spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometries"} Klein geometries
            \.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872, section 1
            ) the theme of the program is described as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p Given a manifold and a transformation group acting on it, to investigate those properties of figures [Gebilde] on that manifold which are invariant under [all] transformations of that group.
          %p
            In modern language this means to consider a group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphisms"} homeomorphisms
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"}> diffeomorphisms
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
            on a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"}> smooth
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            together with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
            of any prescribed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            only emerged at that time, in fact Klein and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"} Sophus Lie
            were in close contact, see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#BirkhoffBennett"} Birkhoff-Bennett
          %p The group of all such transformations
          %blockquote
            %p by which the geometric properties of configurations in space remain entirely unchanged
          %p is called the “Hauptgruppe”, translated to “principal group”.
          %p
            A few lines below in (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872, section 1
            ) is the converse statement
          %blockquote
            %p Given a manifold, and a transformation group acting on it, to study its invariants.
          %p
            Hence to find the figures which are left invariant by a given group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
          %p
            Then in (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872, end of section 5
            ) it says:
          %blockquote
            %p Suppose in space some group or other, the principal group for instance, be given. Let us then select a single configuration, say a point, or a straight line, or even an ellipsoid, etc., and apply to it all the transformations of the principal group. We thus obtain an infinite manifoldness with a number of dimensions in general equal to the number of arbitrary parameters contained in the group, but reducing in special cases, namely, when the configuration originally selected has the property of being transformed into itself by an infinite number of the transformations of the group. Every manifoldness generated in this way may be called, with reference to the generating group, a body.
          %p
            This means in modern language, that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the given group acting on a given space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow X
            is a given subspace (a configuration), then the “body” (“Körper”) generated by this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset"} coset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G/Stab_G(S)
          %p
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
            of that configuration. In the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a point we would now call this the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit"} orbit
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "stabilizer%20group#KleinGeometry"} Stabilizer of shapes – Klein geometry
          %p
            The text goes on to argue that spaces of this form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/Stab_G(S)
            are of fundamental importance:
          %blockquote
            %p If now we desire to base our investigations upon the group, selecting at the same time certain definite configurations as space-elements, and if we wish to represent uniformly things which are of like characteristics, we must evidently choose our space-elements in such a way that their manifoldness either is itself a body or can be decomposed into bodies.
          %p
            Following this, such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+spaces"} coset spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            have come to also be called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometries"} Klein geometries
          %p
            When it was proposed, the Erlangen program served to unify various different kinds of geometry, discovered and studied at that time, into a common framwork. On the other hand, many kinds of geometries without global symmetries are not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometries"} Klein geometries
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            is (in general) not. But a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"}> pseudo
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            is
            %em locally
            (tangentially) modeled on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"}> Minkowski spacetime
            ) and this local model space
            %em is
            a Klein geometry. The generalization of Klein geometry to such local situations is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CartanGeometry"} below
          %h3#in_homotopy_type_theory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            the idea of a group of symmetries preserving a figure inside the larger group of symmetries acting on what the figure is inscribed in is represented by any map of the form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B H \to B G
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            of such a map is the Klein space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
          %h2#refinements_and_generalizations Refinements and generalizations
          %h3#CartanGeometry From local to global geometry – Cartan geometry
          %p
            While many types of geometries (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometries"} Klein geometries
            %em locally
            like Klein geometries. This generalization of Klein geometry is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th local model
                %th global geometry
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+2-geometry"} Klein 2-geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+2-geometry"} Cartan 2-geometry
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Klein+geometry"} higher Klein geometry
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            terminology this corresponds to “locally gauging” the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \hookrightarrow G
            the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            into the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
            is just
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            characterized in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation of gravity
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
                    context
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
